#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fbdcd5f-41de-52fa-9652-3084a5edc01f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Second Term of a Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "78ec85fc-7ed5-5486-bc84-27751b7f70de" :question/math-academy-id "e-14369" :question/problem "If $a_{n+1}=2a_n-3$ with $a_1=4$, then find the value of $a_2$." :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1}=2a_n - 3\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nWe compute $a_2$ by substituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n + 1} &= 2a_{n} - 3 \\\\\na_{1 + 1} &= 2a_{1} - 3 \\\\\na_{2} &= 2a_{1} - 3 \\\\\na_{2} &= 2(4) - 3 \\\\\na_{2} &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a_2=5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100605 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a36f5a6d-8206-5d0f-a3b9-e42c179c0452" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Third Term of a Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "e39ea126-770e-5556-9b16-6a2563c271d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14373" :question/problem "Given the recursive sequence $a_{n+1} = 3a_n-2$ with $a_1=2$, what is the value of $a_3$?" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = 3a_n-2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nWe use this formula to compute the terms $a_2$ and $a_3$ as follows:\n\n- We first compute $a_2$ by substituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula:\n$a_{n + 1}|= 3a_{n} - 2; a_{1 + 1}|= 3a_{1} - 2; a_{2}|= 3a_{1} - 2; a_{2}|= 3(2) - 2; a_{2}|= 4$\n- Then, we compute $a_3$ by substituting $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n$a_{n + 1}|= 3a_{n} - 2; a_{2 + 1}|= 3a_{2} - 2; a_{3}|= 3a_{2} - 2; a_{3}|= 3(4) - 2; a_{3}|= 10$\n\nTherefore, $a_3=10$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100606 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74655b8-92bc-5142-96b7-408643c8ad39" :knowledge-point/title "Recursive Sequences in Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "529c3240-d3a0-5740-a868-dc76f0be5fff" :question/math-academy-id "e-14377" :question/problem "If $f(n+1) = 2f(n) + 1$ with $f(1) = 3$, then what is $f(4)$?" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = 2f(n) + 1\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nf(1) = {\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nWe use this formula to compute the terms $f(2), f(3)$, and $f(4)$ as follows:\n\n- To compute $f(2)$, we substitute $n = 1$ into the recursive formula:\n$f(n + 1)|= 2f(n) + 1; f(1 + 1)|= 2f(1) + 1; f(2)|= 2f(1) + 1; f(2)|= 2(3) + 1; f(2)|= 7$\n- To compute $f(3)$, we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n$f(n + 1)|= 2f(n) + 1; f(2 + 1)|= 2f(2) + 1; f(3)|= 2f(2) + 1; f(3)|= 2(7) + 1; f(3)|= 15$\n- To compute $f(4)$, we substitute $n=3$ into the recursive formula:\n$f(n + 1)|= 2f(n) + 1; f(3 + 1)|= 2f(3) + 1; f(4)|= 2f(3) + 1; f(4)|= 2(15) + 1; f(4)|= 31$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term of the sequence is\n\n$$\nf(4) = 31\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a1bf68a-bdb3-5e76-a587-ff0abdae982f" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Recursive Formula for a Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "86382a53-f250-529c-9980-840d7a614e2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14379" :question/problem "Consider the following sequence:\n$1, \\quad 6, \\quad 11, \\quad 16, \\quad 21, \\quad 26, \\quad \\dots$\nThe recursive formula for this sequence is given by\n$f(n+1) = f(n) + \\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,, \\qquad f(1) = \\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,$.\nFrom left to right, what are the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the first term of the sequence is\n\n$$\nf(1) = {\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nInspecting the terms of the sequence, we see that we always get the next term by adding ${\\color{red}5}$.\n\nTherefore, the recursive rule must be the following:\n\n$$\nf(n+1) = f(n) + \\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{{\\color{red}5}}\\,, \\qquad f(1) = \\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{{\\color{blue}1}}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing values are $5$ and $1$."}]}
